  <section id="honest-answer">
    <h2>The honest answer</h2>
    <p>
      No website, including this one, can tell you that a particular river is safe to swim in today. Water quality changes hour by hour with rainfall, sewage overflows, farming and temperature, and most UK rivers are not routinely monitored for swimmers. What you can do is check the sources that do exist, read the warning signs, and decide how much risk you are willing to accept.
    </p>
    <p>
      Only a small number of UK river stretches have been formally designated as bathing waters, which means they are sampled through the bathing season and given a classification. Most of the places people swim are not designated. For those, you are making your own judgement.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="water-quality">
    <h2>Where to check water quality</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Designated bathing waters.</strong> The Environment Agency's bathing water data (England), Natural Resources Wales, SEPA (Scotland) and the Northern Ireland Department of Agriculture, Environment and Rural Affairs publish classifications and any short-term pollution warnings for designated sites.</li>
      <li><strong>Sewage overflow alerts.</strong> Surfers Against Sewage's Safer Seas &amp; Rivers service and the Rivers Trust's sewage map show reported storm overflow discharges near many rivers. They are a useful early warning, but a clear map does not guarantee clean water.</li>
      <li><strong>Local swim groups.</strong> Wild swimming and open water groups often know which spots are good, which are best avoided after rain, and where access is safe. Treat their advice as local knowledge rather than official guidance.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="after-rain">
    <h2>After rain: the single biggest rule</h2>
    <p>
      Heavy rain washes farm run-off and street drainage into rivers, and it can trigger storm overflows that release diluted sewage. Surfers Against Sewage and the Rivers Trust both advise avoiding swimming for around 48 hours after heavy rainfall, and longer on slow-moving or small rivers. If you cannot see the bottom and the water is brown or unusually high, wait.
    </p>
    <p>
      Illness from open water is usually a stomach upset, but infections of the ears, eyes and skin can also happen. A rarer infection, Weil's disease (leptospirosis), can be spread by water contaminated with rat urine. Cover cuts with waterproof plasters, avoid swallowing water, shower afterwards and see a doctor if you become unwell in the days after a swim, telling them you have been in open water.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="algae">
    <h2>Blue-green algae</h2>
    <p>
      Warm, still, nutrient-rich water can bloom with blue-green algae (cyanobacteria) in summer. A bloom may look like green paint, scum or small flecks on the surface. Some blooms release toxins that can harm people and are often fatal to dogs. If you see one, do not swim, do not let dogs drink or paddle, and report it to the Environment Agency, Natural Resources Wales or SEPA. Slow lowland stretches and backwaters are more likely to bloom than fast, shaded upland rivers.
    </p>
  </section>

