  <section id="cold-water">
    <h2>Cold water shock</h2>
    <p>
      The RNLI's Float to Live advice is based on a hard fact: UK water is cold for much of the year, and water below about 15°C can trigger cold water shock. This is an involuntary gasp and rapid breathing that can cause you to inhale water, and it is a leading factor in accidental drownings even among strong swimmers. If you get into trouble, the RNLI advises leaning back, keeping your airway clear and floating until the shock passes, rather than trying to swim.
    </p>
    <p>
      Enter slowly, do not jump in, keep swims short while you acclimatise, and get out before you are shivering hard. Cold also weakens your arms and legs quickly, which makes swimming back across a current much harder than it looked from the bank.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="physical">
    <h2>Currents, weirs and hidden hazards</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Never swim near weirs, sluices or just upstream of them. The water can recirculate and hold you under even at low levels.</li>
      <li>Fast, high water is dangerous even where it looks gentle. Flow is stronger and colder after rain.</li>
      <li>Submerged branches, shopping trolleys and broken glass are common in lowland rivers. Wear water shoes if you are unsure of the bed.</li>
      <li>Use a brightly coloured tow float so boats and anglers can see you, and swim with someone who can help.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="checklist">
    <h2>A quick checklist</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Has it rained heavily in the last two days? If yes, skip it.</li>
      <li>Check any official bathing water page and the sewage overflow map for your spot.</li>
      <li>Look for algae, scum, dead fish or an unusual smell.</li>
      <li>Check the water temperature and your own tolerance; get in slowly.</li>
      <li>Check for weirs, strong flow and a safe, easy way out.</li>
      <li>Tell someone where you are going and when you will be back.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
